from curriculum_library import dossier

dossier(71,'冲刺：词义与改写','Recognising the same idea',
'Words can change~A notice may say that registration is compulsory while a question asks whether people must sign up. The vocabulary differs, but the obligation is the same. Matching exact words is therefore insufficient.||Meaning can change too~Two expressions may look similar without being equivalent. May apply does not mean will receive, and reduced price does not mean free. Small differences can determine the answer.||Use the surrounding sentence~An unfamiliar word can sometimes be interpreted from a contrast or example. However, context does not justify inventing a precise meaning when the passage provides too little evidence.||Practise both directions~Explain a sentence in simpler words, then check whether any condition or degree of certainty was lost. Good paraphrase preserves the original claim instead of making it stronger.',
'A booking condition~The phrase subject to availability means a request depends on remaining capacity. It does not guarantee that a place exists when someone completes a form.||A deadline condition~No later than Friday sets an inclusive latest time. It is different from sometime after Friday. The precise time on Friday may still matter if the notice specifies noon.||A cost condition~The fee covers materials indicates that those materials are included. It does not establish whether travel, meals or optional equipment are included as well.||A scope condition~Available to existing members limits eligibility. A new applicant cannot assume the same offer applies unless the text extends it explicitly.',
'Checking a paraphrase~Learner: The notice says applicants may receive support. Can I write that everyone will get funding? Coach: No. You have changed possibility into certainty.||A better version~Learner: Eligible applicants can be considered for support. Coach: That is closer, provided the text also specifies eligibility.||Another distinction~Learner: The fee is reduced, so attendance is free. Coach: Reduced means lower, not necessarily zero.||Practice~Coach: Keep the amount, condition and certainty level when restating an idea. Simpler language must still preserve the meaning.',
'A museum offer~Residents qualify for discounted entry on weekdays after three. The offer does not apply on public holidays.||Proof~A current document showing an address in the district is required. A visitor accompanying a resident does not automatically qualify.||Bookings~Advance booking is recommended, but the notice does not make it compulsory. Tickets may be available at the door.||Interpretation~A recommendation to book is not a guarantee of availability, and a discount is not an offer of free admission.')

dossier(72,'冲刺：目标检查','Assessing the remaining gap',
'A fixed goal~The learner\'s goal remains an overall IELTS General score of 6.5. Intermediate milestones help review progress; they do not lower the final goal whenever practice becomes difficult.||Evidence before prediction~A recent four-skill record is more useful than an old test or a long completion streak. Sources and conditions should be visible, especially where scores are informal estimates.||A component profile~The same overall average can arise from different skill combinations. A learner should examine each component and any requirements of the organization receiving the result, rather than assume an overall score satisfies every purpose.||A decision under constraints~If evidence remains below the target, the choices include changing emphasis, increasing time if possible or extending the preparation period. Keeping the original date does not make missing skills disappear.',
'A final-month plan~I have stronger reading evidence than speaking evidence. For the next fortnight I will keep one reading review session while using two main-practice slots for spontaneous speaking and feedback.||Protecting core practice~I will not abandon listening or writing completely. The change is a shift in emphasis, with brief maintenance tasks for stronger areas and more targeted work on the weakest evidence.||A feedback condition~If AI suggests a speaking band without hearing audio, I will treat its comments only as feedback on the transcript. Pronunciation requires audio evidence, and even audio-based estimates are not official results.||A review date~After two weeks I will attempt unfamiliar tasks again. If the gap remains substantial, I will revise the schedule instead of interpreting repeated familiar answers as proof of readiness.',
'Reviewing the target~Coach: Your target is 6.5 overall. Do you also have an external minimum for each skill? Learner: I need to check the organization\'s requirements.||Separating assumptions~Coach: The project suggests 6.0 per skill as a training preference. That is not a universal IELTS rule.||Assessing evidence~Learner: Two scores are AI estimates. Coach: Keep that label visible and avoid presenting the combined result as an official achievement.||Next action~Coach: Use the next practice cycle to strengthen the least reliable evidence, then reassess the remaining time.',
'A course completion notice~Completing all modules produces a record of study activity. It does not certify a particular external examination score.||Assessment evidence~Learners may attach official results or mock-test records with dates and sources. Informal estimates remain separately labelled.||Advice~The course recommends reviewing all four skills before making an examination decision. A strong result in one skill is not a substitute for the others.||Scheduling~Learners who need more time may continue reviewing after the calendar ends. The end of the schedule does not erase saved work or prevent further practice.')

dossier(73,'冲刺：听力薄弱点','Working with unfamiliar voices',
'A comfortable routine~A learner repeatedly practised with one synthetic voice at a slow speed. Recognition improved, but unfamiliar natural speech still felt difficult. The practice had not exposed the learner to enough variation.||Introducing variation~Short recordings from different speakers were added gradually. The learner first identified the situation and key information, then reviewed difficult phrases. Variation did not require starting with the longest or fastest recording available.||Keeping a fair record~Slowed or repeated listening remained useful practice, but was labelled separately from a first independent attempt. Otherwise, the progress record could overstate performance under examination conditions.||A focused review~The learner distinguished sound-recognition problems from unknown vocabulary and lost attention. Each problem led to a different response: focused replay, meaning work or shorter listening segments.',
'An unfamiliar interview~At first I understood individual words but lost the main point. I had tried to write too much and stopped listening while finishing my notes.||A different note style~On the next recording I wrote only names, changes and reasons. This left more attention for the speaker\'s developing argument. A full transcript was unnecessary for the task.||Checking the difficult section~After answering, I compared the transcript with what I thought I had heard. A linking phrase had signalled a contrast that I missed.||A transfer check~I then used a different recording with a similar contrast. Replaying the original until it sounded clear was useful, but the new recording tested whether the lesson transferred.',
'A listening review~Coach: What made this recording difficult? Learner: I knew the words, but the speaker linked them and I missed where one ended.||Targeted practice~Coach: Replay a short phrase, compare it with the transcript, and say it in a natural rhythm. Then listen again without reading.||Avoiding overclaiming~Learner: I understand it perfectly now. Coach: That is progress on this recording. Try an unfamiliar example before concluding that the broader difficulty is resolved.||Conditions~Coach: Keep first-listen results separate from repeated and slowed practice.',
'A listening collection~A public learning site provides recordings with transcripts at several levels. Transcripts are learning aids, not a requirement to read while listening.||Independent attempts~Learners can first answer comprehension questions without opening the text, then use it to locate missed information.||Replay~Repeated listening helps analysis, but results after many repetitions should not be compared directly with first-attempt scores.||Source notes~Recordings should retain their original source links and usage conditions. Access to a recording does not automatically permit redistribution.')

dossier(74,'冲刺：阅读时间管理','When to move on',
'A costly habit~A reader spent several minutes on the first unfamiliar word in a passage. Later questions remained unanswered even though many concerned information the reader could have located quickly.||Task-led reading~The learner practised identifying what each question required before deciding how closely to read. Some tasks needed a broad understanding; others required precise conditions or details.||A provisional answer~When evidence remained unclear, the learner marked the item for review and continued. Moving on was a time-management choice, not a claim that the question was unimportant.||Reviewing the pattern~After the session, the learner examined whether time was lost through vocabulary gaps, repeated rereading or uncertainty about instructions. A general instruction to read faster would not identify the cause.',
'A timed attempt~I completed the first section carefully but left too little time for the longer final section. My accuracy on answered items looked good, while my total result showed the cost of unfinished work.||A revised allocation~In the next attempt I set checkpoints rather than spending the same amount on every paragraph. I allowed more time for the longer section and tracked questions needing a return visit.||Keeping comprehension~I did not simply scan every line faster. For questions involving a condition or comparison, careful reading still mattered. The change was deciding where to invest that attention.||Evaluating the change~I compared total correct answers and unfinished items on a different task. The aim was better overall performance, not an impressive speed figure without understanding.',
'During review~Coach: You spent eight minutes on one matching question. What kept you there? Learner: Two options seemed possible, and I reread the same sentences.||A missing condition~Coach: Which requirement distinguishes them? Learner: One needs weekend availability. I overlooked that.||A next strategy~Coach: Check the requirements explicitly, then move on if evidence remains unclear. Return after easier items rather than consuming the whole section.||Measuring success~Coach: Track unanswered questions as well as errors. Both affect the usefulness of the strategy.',
'A reading workshop~Participants complete an unfamiliar task under a fixed limit. They may mark uncertain items but cannot extend the limit for a preferred question.||Review records~After time ends, they note which questions were unfinished and where excessive rereading occurred.||Second-stage analysis~They then work without the timer to distinguish knowledge gaps from time pressure. These results are stored separately.||A later check~The strategy is tested on new material. Improved performance on the original text alone is not treated as a comparable timed result.')

dossier(75,'冲刺：写作修改','Revise the message first',
'A polished but incomplete letter~A learner corrected spelling and added formal phrases, yet the letter still omitted the requested alternative date. Surface accuracy had improved without completing the communication task.||A review order~The learner first checked purpose and required information, then paragraph organization, and finally sentence-level errors. This order reduced the chance of polishing text that would later need major changes.||Prioritising recurring errors~Feedback identified repeated tense and article problems. The learner selected two patterns to practise in new sentences rather than trying to eliminate every possible error at once.||Independent revision~The learner rewrote the letter before viewing a model answer. Comparing afterwards helped identify differences without turning the task into copying a fluent example.',
'A first draft problem~My essay argued that public transport should improve, but the question asked whether fares should be free. I had discussed a related topic without making a clear judgement about the actual proposal.||A structural revision~I rewrote the introduction to state a position, then developed affordability and funding in separate paragraphs. I removed a paragraph about road design that did not support the main argument.||A language revision~Only after reorganizing did I correct repeated sentence errors. This saved time because some awkward sentences disappeared with the irrelevant paragraph.||A transfer task~The following week I answered a different policy question. Before writing, I restated the instruction in plain language to avoid repeating the same task-response mistake.',
'A feedback discussion~Coach: Your letter explains the problem clearly. What action do you want the recipient to take? Learner: I want a replacement appointment.||A missing detail~Coach: Then offer suitable times or ask them to propose one. At present the reader knows you are unhappy but not how to resolve it.||Language feedback~Learner: Should I add more formal vocabulary? Coach: First make the request complete. Plain, accurate language is preferable to an unclear request with elaborate phrases.||Revision~Coach: Write the new request yourself, then compare it with the original purpose.',
'A writing review checklist~Check whether every part of the prompt has been addressed. A minimum word count does not guarantee a complete response.||Organization~Each paragraph should have a clear role and a logical relationship to the next. More linking expressions do not automatically create coherence.||Language~Review recurring grammar and word-choice problems using examples from the draft. Avoid replacing simple words solely because alternatives appear more advanced.||Evidence~Save the original and revised versions with feedback. A polished final version alone does not show how independently it was produced.')

dossier(76,'冲刺：口语灵活性','Answering the question you hear',
'A memorized mismatch~A candidate prepared a detailed description of a holiday. When asked about a useful journey, the candidate delivered the holiday speech without explaining usefulness. Fluency did not compensate for weak relevance.||Listening to the focus~The key distinction might involve usefulness, difficulty, change or personal importance. Identifying that focus helps select relevant details rather than reciting everything known about the topic.||Developing naturally~A short direct answer can be followed by a reason and example. If a follow-up changes the topic, the speaker can adapt rather than forcing the conversation back to the prepared material.||Using feedback~Recordings reveal whether answers address the prompt, develop ideas and remain understandable. Transcript-only feedback cannot establish how pronunciation or delivery affected the listener.',
'A practice response~A useful journey I made was a visit to a training center in another town. It was not a holiday, but it helped me decide whether to apply for a course.||A relevant detail~I spoke to current students and saw the equipment they used. The website had described the course, but the visit answered practical questions about the timetable and travel.||A consequence~I chose a different course closer to home because the daily journey would have been difficult. The visit was useful even though it did not lead to enrollment there.||A broader reflection~Direct experience can reveal details missing from advertisements. However, a single visit may not show what ordinary days are like, so I would also ask specific questions.',
'A speaking exchange~Coach: Describe a useful journey. Learner: I visited a training center to decide whether its course suited me.||An unexpected question~Coach: Could you have learned the same things online? Learner: Some information was available, but I needed to understand the journey and see the practical facilities.||A challenge~Coach: Does visiting always lead to a better decision? Learner: Not always. A well-organized open day may look different from an ordinary class.||Feedback~Coach: Your answer stayed relevant and included a qualification. Now practise a different journey without reusing the same story.',
'A discussion task~Speakers describe an occasion when they changed a decision after receiving new information. They should explain the original plan, the new evidence and the revised choice.||Follow-up questions~Partners ask whether the change was difficult and whether the speaker would act differently now.||Preparation~One minute of keyword notes is allowed. Reading a complete written script is not part of the task.||Review~Feedback distinguishes content, organization and delivery. Pronunciation comments require listening to the recording rather than reading notes alone.')

dossier(77,'终检准备','Protecting unfamiliar evidence',
'Keep material separate~A final check needs tasks the learner has not already studied. If the passage and answer explanations were used in lessons, the resulting score includes familiarity and cannot serve the same purpose.||Record assistance~Translation, transcript access, pausing and outside correction should be recorded. These can support learning, but they change what the attempt demonstrates about independent performance.||Use appropriate sources~Official sample tasks and legitimately obtained practice materials provide clearer examination context than a short self-authored quiz. Their instructions and scoring guidance should be followed.||Interpret the result carefully~One performance may be affected by fatigue, anxiety or topic familiarity. Repeated unfamiliar checks provide more useful evidence, while formal certification still requires the actual examination.',
'Preparing the record~I create a separate entry for each skill with source, date, timing and support used. I avoid combining results from very different periods into a supposedly current profile.||A writing limitation~My writing feedback comes from AI, so I keep the original draft and label the estimate. I ask for comments tied to the official criteria, but do not describe the result as an examiner\'s judgement.||An endurance limitation~My daily limit permits individual reading and writing sections, not a complete consecutive test. I record this openly rather than assuming the same performance would continue across several hours.||A decision point~If the evidence is inconsistent, I plan another unfamiliar check or more targeted practice. The calendar\'s approaching end is a reason to review carefully, not to inflate the conclusion.',
'A final-check briefing~Coach: Have you seen this reading before? Learner: I used its first passage last month. Coach: Then choose another section for independent evidence.||Handling help~Learner: Can I use a dictionary afterwards? Coach: Yes, during review. Keep that separate from the timed attempt and do not revise the original score.||Scoring~Learner: Should I replace my first answers with corrected ones? Coach: Save corrections as a new result so both learning and initial performance remain visible.||Conclusion~Coach: We are gathering evidence of current ability, not trying to make the progress page look perfect.',
'An assessment archive~A learner stores unused practice materials separately from completed exercises. File labels indicate whether a task has been opened or answered.||Answer keys~Keys remain closed until the attempt ends. Reading explanations beforehand changes the task from assessment to guided practice.||Repeat attempts~Repeats are allowed for learning but retain a previously-seen label. Their results do not replace the independent first attempt.||Source records~Each task includes its origin and any applicable scoring notes. The learner does not invent an IELTS band conversion for a short custom quiz.')

dossier(78,'终检与后续学习','A conclusion supported by evidence',
'What completion means~Finishing a seventy-eight-week course demonstrates sustained participation. It may also produce substantial improvement, but completion alone does not establish a specific examination result. The final review must examine actual performance.||A complete skill profile~The learner reviews unfamiliar listening and reading attempts, timed writing and spontaneous speaking. Sources, assistance and feedback quality remain visible so stronger and weaker evidence are not confused.||Two possible next steps~If evidence is close to the target across skills, the learner can consider the appropriate examination decision. If important gaps remain, a focused extension is more useful than repeating the whole course from the beginning.||Continuing after the plan~Saved drafts, recordings and error patterns provide a basis for ongoing study. The calendar ends, but access to materials and revision should remain available. Learning is not erased by a delayed target date.',
'A final reflection~At the beginning I relied heavily on translation and prepared sentences. I can now understand more unfamiliar material and explain everyday problems more independently. These changes matter even before an official score is available.||An honest weakness~I still need more practice organizing unfamiliar arguments quickly. My revised essays are stronger than my first timed drafts. The difference shows where assistance helps and where independent skill needs further work.||A focused extension~I will use the next month for two writing cycles each week while maintaining listening and speaking. Each cycle includes an unfamiliar prompt, feedback and a new transfer task.||A measurable review~At the end of the month I will compare new timed drafts using the same criteria. I will not measure success only by the number of pages written or the length of my study streak.',
'The final conversation~Coach: What evidence best supports your progress? Learner: My unfamiliar reading attempts and recordings show clearer understanding and expression than the baseline.||A remaining uncertainty~Coach: Which area has the least reliable evidence? Learner: Writing, because most feedback is informal and several drafts were revised with help.||A next plan~Coach: Keep the target, but focus the extension on independent writing and comparable feedback. There is no need to restart every completed lesson.||A record of limits~Coach: The report should describe progress, remaining gaps and source quality. Only an official result should be labelled as an official IELTS achievement.',
'A continuing-study policy~After the planned end date, learners can still open previous weeks, review mistakes and export their records. The course does not lock completed material.||A revised schedule~An extension can target selected skills without deleting earlier progress. Calendar position and learning completion remain separate measures.||Evidence updates~New assessments are added with dates and sources. Older results remain available for comparison and are not silently overwritten.||Final interpretation~A course report summarizes learning activity and submitted evidence. It cannot guarantee future performance or replace an official examination certificate.')
